I'll solve this inequality directly. The key idea is to find a per-term bound of the form $\frac{a^3+3b^3}{5a+b} \geq pa^2+qb^2$ with $p+q=\tfrac23$, so that summing cyclically gives the desired $\tfrac23(a^2+b^2+c^2)$.

---

**Proof.** We claim that for all positive reals $a,b$,
$$\frac{a^3+3b^3}{5a+b}\geq -\frac{1}{36}\,a^2+\frac{25}{36}\,b^2. \tag{$\star$}$$

Setting $t=b/a>0$ and dividing both sides by $a^2$, this is equivalent to
$$\frac{1+3t^3}{5+t}\geq -\frac{1}{36}+\frac{25}{36}\,t^2.$$

Clearing the (positive) denominator $36(5+t)$, this becomes
$$36(1+3t^3)\geq (5+t)(-1+25t^2),$$
i.e.,
$$36+108t^3 \geq -5-t+125t^2+25t^3,$$
i.e.,
$$83t^3-125t^2+t+41\geq 0.$$

We factor the cubic. Checking $t=1$: $83-125+1+41=0$, so $(t-1)$ divides it. Performing the division,
$$83t^3-125t^2+t+41=(t-1)(83t^2-42t-41).$$

The quadratic $83t^2-42t-41$ has discriminant $42^2+4\cdot83\cdot41=1764+13612=15376=124^2$, giving roots $\frac{42\pm124}{166}$, i.e., $1$ and $-\frac{41}{83}$. Hence
$$83t^2-42t-41=(t-1)(83t+41),$$
and therefore
$$83t^3-125t^2+t+41=(t-1)^2(83t+41).$$

For $t>0$ we have $(t-1)^2\geq 0$ and $83t+41>0$, so the expression is non-negative. This proves $(\star)$, with equality iff $t=1$, i.e., $a=b$.

Now apply $(\star)$ cyclically:
$$\frac{a^3+3b^3}{5a+b}\geq -\frac{1}{36}a^2+\frac{25}{36}b^2,$$
$$\frac{b^3+3c^3}{5b+c}\geq -\frac{1}{36}b^2+\frac{25}{36}c^2,$$
$$\frac{c^3+3a^3}{5c+a}\geq -\frac{1}{36}c^2+\frac{25}{36}a^2.$$

Summing these three inequalities,
$$\frac{a^3+3b^3}{5a+b}+\frac{b^3+3c^3}{5b+c}+\frac{c^3+3a^3}{5c+a}\geq \left(-\frac{1}{36}+\frac{25}{36}\right)(a^2+b^2+c^2)=\frac{24}{36}(a^2+b^2+c^2)=\frac{2}{3}(a^2+b^2+c^2).$$

Equality holds throughout iff $a=b$, $b=c$, $c=a$, i.e., $a=b=c$. $\blacksquare$

